<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · Sacred · 1876 · Oceania</p><h1 class="sg-title">God Defend New Zealand</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi>God Defend New Zealand</bdi> / <bdi>Aotearoa</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A poem from the 1870s, set to music in a contest, that became one of New Zealand's two national anthems in 1977.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Thomas Bracken (English); Thomas Henry Smith (Māori)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>John Joseph Woods</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>New Zealanders, Pākehā and Māori</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English, Māori</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Dunedin, New Zealand</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>poem written in the 1870s; music 1876; Māori version 1878</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (English words by Thomas Bracken (1870s; in the public domain since 1949), Māori words by Thomas Henry Smith (1878), music by John Joseph Woods (1876; in the public domain since the 1980s))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-defend-new-zealand">God Defend New Zealand</a>&quot; began as a poem written in the 1870s by Thomas Bracken of Dunedin, an Irish-born immigrant raised in Victoria, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. In 1876 The Saturday Advertiser held a competition to set it to music, judged by three prominent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a> musicians, with a prize of ten guineas. It was won by John Joseph Woods of Lawrence, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otago">Otago</a>, born in Van Diemen's Land, who wrote the melody in a single sitting on the evening after he heard of the competition. The song was first performed at the Queen's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> on Princes Street, Dunedin, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christmas">Christmas</a> Day 1876, and the sheet music was published in February 1878.</p><p>The Māori version was made in 1878 by Thomas Henry Smith of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a>, a judge of the Native Land Court, at the request of Premier George Edward Grey, and printed under the title &quot;Aotearoa&quot; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otago">Otago</a> newspapers in October 1878. It is not a direct translation of the English, and the two have slightly different meanings. When the anthem was published as the national hymn in 1940, several errors entered the Māori text, among them a variant spelling of one verb that many later versions repeat; the Ministry for Culture and Heritage keeps the 1878 spelling.</p><p>In 1897 Premier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-seddon">Richard Seddon</a> presented a copy of the words and music to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>. The song grew in popularity in the early 20th century, and in 1940 the government bought its copyright and made it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>'s national hymn in time for the centennial celebrations. It was played at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> Games from 1950, and at the Olympics for the first time in 1952, alongside the royal anthem, after Yvette Williams won the long jump. After it was played again at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> Olympics in 1972, a campaign began to make it the national anthem. In 1975 the new Television One opened each day's broadcasting with it, and its network controller, Bill Munro, decided on his own to close with it as well in place of the British anthem. In 1976 Garth Henry Latta of Dunedin petitioned Parliament, and on 21 November 1977, with the consent of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-muldoon">Robert Muldoon</a>'s government gazetted it as New Zealand's second national anthem, equal in status with &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the Queen</a>&quot;.</p><p>In 1979 the Minister of Internal Affairs, Allan Highet, announced an official arrangement for massed singing by Maxwell Fernie, a semitone lower than Woods's original in A-flat major. For Expo 88 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brisbane">Brisbane</a>, Alan Slater made an up-tempo arrangement in both languages, sung by Annie Crummer, Peter Morgan, Dalvanius Prime and the Pātea Māori Club; TVNZ opened its broadcasts with it from 1988 to 1995. Until the 1990s usually only the first English verse was sung. Vicky Lee and Cyndi Joe sang it in both Māori and English at a rugby league test in 1992, and after Hinewehi Mohi sang only the Māori first verse at the 1999 Rugby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifa-world-cup">World Cup</a> and stirred public debate, it became the custom to sing the first verse in Māori and then in English.</p><p>Legally the two anthems have equal status, but &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-defend-new-zealand">God Defend New Zealand</a>&quot; is the one more commonly used. The 1977 guidelines advise it whenever <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>'s national identity is to be stressed, and the royal anthem when the monarch, a member of the royal family or the governor-general is present or loyalty to the Crown is to be stressed. Some have criticised its old-fashioned wording and a melody hard to sing at the original pitch, but no widely accepted replacement has been found. In 2013 the original score and words were added to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Memory of the World register for New Zealand; the documents are kept at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a> Central City Library.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A prayer to God to guard <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and its people. The mood is solemn and hymn-like, each verse closing on the plea of the title. The Māori version follows the same prayer in its own words rather than as a translation.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God of Nations at Thy feet,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In the bonds of love we meet,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hear our voices, we entreat,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God defend our free land.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Guard Pacific's triple star</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>From the shafts of strife and war,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Make her praises heard afar,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God defend New Zealand.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Men of every creed and race,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Gather here before Thy face,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Asking Thee to bless this place,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God defend our free land.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>From dissension, envy, hate,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And corruption guard our State,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Make our country good and great,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God defend New Zealand.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Peace, not war, shall be our boast,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But, should foes assail our coast,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Make us then a mighty host,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God defend our free land.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Lord of battles in Thy might,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Put our enemies to flight,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Let our cause be just and right,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God defend New Zealand.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Let our love for Thee increase,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>May Thy blessings never cease,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Give us plenty, give us peace,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God defend our free land.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>From dishonour and from shame,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Guard our country's spotless name,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Crown her with immortal fame,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God defend New Zealand.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>May our mountains ever be</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Freedom's ramparts on the sea,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Make us faithful unto Thee,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God defend our free land.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Guide her in the nation's van,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Preaching love and truth to man,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Working out Thy glorious plan,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>God defend New Zealand.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Thomas Bracken (1876). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/God_Defend_New_Zealand" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
